          {
            "proposalId": "part-2-repair-8",
            "fullIndependentRationale": "釗 = Zhao; both occurrences fixed. Relationships unchanged. 毀丸都而歸 records a distinct event: Huang, the subject of the whole sentence, returned after destroying Wandu. The paired literal keeps it ('destroyed Wandu and returned'), but no clause in the idiomatic field conveys it. Adding 'and returned' with Huang as the shared subject restores the omitted event. It keeps the approved Zhao spelling and every other event (digging the tomb, carrying off the corpse, mother, wife and treasures, plundering 50,000+, burning the palaces, razing Wandu) unchanged. This supersedes my earlier style-level note on this omission. No other prior decision changes.",
            "nativeRationale": "The Chinese explicitly records Huang returning (而歸). Retain the independently accepted Zhao spelling and restore the return event in the idiomatic field, matching the literal field and primary text.",
            "fullEvidencePreserved": true
          },
